// PosixMmap — the `mmap(NULL, size, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE, MAP_ANONYMOUS|MAP_PRIVATE, -1, 0)`
// parameters `osAllocPages` bottoms out in on every POSIX target. Written ONCE and consumed by BOTH
// Linux isels (x64's `X64LinuxRuntime`/`StdToX64Conversion` and arm64's `StdToArm64Conversion`), so the
// ABI is one fact rather than two copies with nothing checking them (OPEN #83).
//
// Four of these are POSIX-UNIVERSAL — identical on Darwin and Linux (base, prot, fd, offset). The fifth,
// `MmapAnonPrivateLinux`, is the LINUX flag word; Darwin's differs (`MAP_ANON` is 0x1000 there, 0x20 on
// Linux, over a shared `MAP_PRIVATE` of 0x2 — so 0x1002 vs 0x22), and that Darwin word stays with the
// arm64-macOS isel (`StdToArm64Conversion.MmapAnonPrivateMacos`), the only target that needs it — x64
// never targets macOS.
//
// ⭐ MAP_ANON IS WHAT MAKES THE ZEROING CONTRACT TRUE: an anonymous mapping is kernel-zeroed on first
// touch, which is the slab's zeroing contract (exactly as Windows's MEM_COMMIT is). A MAP_ANON mapping
// must name NO file, which is why `fd` is -1.
export let MmapKernelChoosesBase = 0x0  // addr = NULL: the kernel picks the base.
export let MmapProtReadWrite = 0x3  // PROT_READ (0x1) | PROT_WRITE (0x2)
export let MmapAnonPrivateLinux = 0x22  // Linux: MAP_ANONYMOUS (0x20) | MAP_PRIVATE (0x2)
export let MmapAnonFd = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF  // fd = -1 (two's-complement): MAP_ANON names no file.
export let MmapNoOffset = 0x0  // offset = 0 (ignored for an anonymous mapping).

// --- F1a: the `madvise` advice that DECOMMITS a range (drops its physical backing, keeps the mapping) ---
//
// ⭐ **THE ADVICE NUMBER IS A LINUX FACT AND IS ARCH-INVARIANT, WHICH IS WHY IT SITS BESIDE THE MMAP FLAGS
// RATHER THAN IN EITHER BACKEND.** Linux defines the whole `madvise` advice table in `asm-generic`, so
// `MADV_DONTNEED` is 4 on x86-64 and on AArch64 alike — one fact, consumed by both Linux isels, exactly like
// `MmapAnonPrivateLinux` above. (The SYSCALL NUMBER is not arch-invariant and is therefore prefixed and kept
// per-backend: 28 on x86-64, 233 on AArch64.)
//
// ⚠ **DARWIN'S ANSWER IS A DIFFERENT ADVICE, NOT A DIFFERENT NUMBER FOR THIS ONE.** `MADV_FREE` (5) is what
// decommits on Darwin, and it has WEAKER SEMANTICS: a page may come back still DIRTY. That constant lives with
// the arm64-macOS isel (`StdToArm64Conversion.MadviseFreeMacos`), the only backend that targets macOS —
// precisely the split `MmapAnonPrivateMacos` already makes, and for the same reason. Sharing one name across the
// two would hide a semantic difference behind a numeric one; see `osDecommitPages` for what rests on it.
export let MadviseDontNeedLinux = 0x4  // Linux: MADV_DONTNEED — drop the pages; the next touch refaults a ZERO page.

// --- F1c: the two `mmap` parameters that turn one page source into RESERVE and COMMIT ---
//
// ⭐⭐ **POSIX HAS NO COMMIT CALL, SO BOTH HALVES OF THE SPLIT ARE `mmap` WITH DIFFERENT ARGUMENTS.** A
// reservation is `mmap(NULL, size, PROT_NONE, MAP_ANON|MAP_PRIVATE, -1, 0)` — the kernel records the range and
// backs nothing, and a touch faults because the pages permit nothing. A commit is the SAME mapping call over an
// address the reservation already owns, with real protection and `MAP_FIXED`: it replaces that sub-range with
// fresh anonymous pages. Both are Go's, verbatim (`vendor/go/src/runtime/mem_linux.go:163` and `:172`,
// `mem_darwin.go:57` and `:67`), which is why neither needs a new import or a new syscall number — `mmap` is
// already the page source both isels call.
//
// ⚠ **THESE TWO ARE POSIX-UNIVERSAL, UNLIKE THE ANON-PRIVATE WORD ABOVE.** `PROT_NONE` is 0 and `MAP_FIXED` is
// 0x10 on Linux and on Darwin alike, so they sit here rather than being split per-OS the way
// `MmapAnonPrivateLinux`/`MmapAnonPrivateMacos` are. Each isel ORs `MmapFixed` into ITS OWN anon-private word
// at the one site that materializes the immediate, so the per-OS fact stays stated once.
export let MmapProtNone = 0x0  // PROT_NONE: reserved address space, and a touch of it FAULTS.
export let MmapFixed = 0x10  // MAP_FIXED — map EXACTLY at the given address, REPLACING whatever was there.

// --- The SHARED mapping: the one flag that makes two processes see the same bytes ---
//
// ⭐ **`MAP_SHARED` IS THE WHOLE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN A PAGE SOURCE AND A CHANNEL.** Every mapping above is
// `MAP_PRIVATE`, whose writes are copy-on-write and therefore invisible to anyone else; a named segment two
// processes both map is the opposite of that by definition, so the ring's mapping carries this flag INSTEAD
// of the anon-private word — and names a descriptor rather than `MmapAnonFd`, because there is a shared
// object to name.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS POSIX-UNIVERSAL (0x1 on Darwin and on Linux alike)**, unlike the anon-private word two blocks
// up, which is why it stands here unqualified.
export let MmapShared = 0x1
